course: dictionary
lesson: 5862
title: Many meanings of man
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one you need. Most often, a man is a grown-up male person, and men is the plural: two men, many men.
explain: Sometimes man means people in general, which is an older use. «The Head Chief says Taffy has made a great invention which men will one day call writing.» Here men means people. The whole of humankind will one day call it writing, not just grown-up males.
explain: Man can also be a doing word. To man something means to work at it or be in charge of it, as in man the desk. Ask yourself: is it a person, a thing in a game, or an action? The clues will show you which.
fact: A man is a grown-up male person, and the plural is men.
fact: Man can mean people in general, an older use: «Man has walked on the Moon.»
fact: To man something means to work at it or be in charge of it, as in man the desk.
fact: In games, a man is a piece, such as a chess man.
fact: For hundreds of years, people said «the man upstairs» instead of God's name, because saying it aloud felt rude or unlucky.
exercise: In «Man has walked on the Moon.», what does man mean? | A chess piece | *People in general | A grown-up male person | To work at something
exercise: In «Ravi was asked to man the desk», what does man mean? | A grown-up male person | Men, more than one | *To be in charge of, or work at, something | A chess piece
exercise: «The old man sat by the fire and told a tale.» What kind of word is man here? | A piece in a game | A verb, a doing word | A word for a long rest | *A noun, a grown-up male person
test: What is the plural of man? | *men | mans | manning | manned
test: In a chess game, what is a man? | A word for a long rest | A place to sit | *A piece you move on the board | A grown-up male person
exam: Which sentence uses man to mean people in general? | «The postman rang the bell twice.» | «The old man sat by the fire.» | «The fireman climbed the ladder.» | *«Man has walked on the Moon.»
